# The browser bundle, as an image whose only job is to carry it.
#
# The bundle used to be built by hand and committed: 5 MB into git history per
# release, and a step a person had to remember. Twice in one day somebody changed
# the simulation core and did not rebuild it, and the second time a player joining
# Chaos was shown DESTROYED on a healthy server, because the client's compiled core
# read a wire the server had stopped writing. A build step that depends on
# remembering is the same class of thing as a version number that depends on
# remembering, and we had already been bitten by that one.
#
# CI builds it and publishes it here. The context is the built page alone, because
# building it needs a JDK, bob.jar and Defold's remote build server, none of which
# belong on a game host or in this image.
#
# Deliberately not the Caddy image. Baking the page into Caddy would mean
# recreating Caddy on every client release, and recreating Caddy severs every
# WebSocket it carries -- so a change to a menu label would disconnect everybody
# mid-match. This is a payload that copies itself into a volume Caddy is already
# serving, so Caddy never restarts.
#
# No entrypoint: what to do with the payload is in docker-compose.yml, where
# somebody reading the deployment can see it rather than having to inspect an
# image to find out.
FROM busybox:stable-musl
COPY . /page/
